---
import Window from "../product/Window.astro"
import Section from "../site/Section.astro"
import SectionHead from "../site/SectionHead.astro"
import type { DocLinks } from "../../lib/doc-links"

interface Props {
  links: DocLinks
}

const { links } = Astro.props

const QUERY_FORMS = [
  "service.name:checkout and log.level:error",
  "lucene:service.name:checkout AND NOT log.level:info",
  '{"query": {"match_phrase": {"message": "timeout"}}}',
]
---

<Section>
  <SectionHead
    title="From Kibana or OpenSearch Dashboards: same clusters, same queries."
    lead="Nothing moves. SlashObserve reads the index patterns you already have, in the language you already type."
    space={32}
  />
  <div class="cols2 kibana">
    <div class="kibana-copy">
      <div>
        <h3 class="h3">Connect the same clusters and indices</h3>
        <p class="p">
          An Elasticsearch or OpenSearch datasource is one index pattern, the data view you had in Discover: a pattern
          such as logs-*, a data stream or an alias, with its time field. Basic auth, a bearer token or an API key.
          Elasticsearch 8 and 9, OpenSearch 2 and 3; the health check names the flavour and version it found.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="h3">KQL carries over</h3>
        <p class="p">
          Type the KQL you typed in Discover: service.name:checkout and log.level:error, a quoted phrase, a range, a
          wildcard, or and not with parentheses. The server compiles it to Query DSL. It is a strict subset of Kibana's KQL, so a
          query Kibana accepts may be refused: nested field queries are, with a message, and a mistake is marked at the character. Ask in
          plain language instead and the agent drafts the KQL for you.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="h3">Lucene and Query DSL when you want them</h3>
        <p class="p">
          Start a query with lucene: for Lucene syntax, or paste a {"{...}"} body for the Query DSL, aggregations included.
          A datasource can make Lucene its default instead of KQL. Lint checks all three before anything runs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="hint">
        Saved objects are not imported. Dashboards, saved searches and visualizations from Kibana or OpenSearch
        Dashboards stay where they are; describe the dashboard you want and the agent builds it against the same index.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="kibana-side">
      <Window label="The three query forms on an Elasticsearch or OpenSearch datasource">
        <div class="term">{QUERY_FORMS.map((line) => <span class="tl">{line}</span>)}</div>
      </Window>
      {links.elasticsearch && <a class="arrow-link" href={links.elasticsearch}>Connect Elasticsearch →</a>}
      {links.opensearch && <a class="arrow-link" href={links.opensearch}>Connect OpenSearch →</a>}
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .kibana {
    align-items: start;
  }

  .kibana-copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 28px;
  }

  .kibana-copy .p {
    margin-top: 6px;
  }

  .kibana-side {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 16px;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .kibana-side :global(.win) {
    align-self: stretch;
  }

  .arrow-link {
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
